# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This guide covers how teams select a PostgreSQL engine version and prepare an
**in-place major-version upgrade**, such as PostgreSQL 14 to 17. An in-place upgrade
updates the existing server and all its databases, retaining the server name and
connection endpoint.

## Shared default

Check the shared PostgreSQL server manifest in SDS or CNP for the configured default. Teams can explicitly select an engine version using spec.version in their server patch.


The shared manifests have been restored to their pre-PR configuration to retain
compatibility with existing team patches. Teams own engine upgrades. A future
shared API change requires matching patch APIs in the same validated rollout;
unrelated ASO APIs do not need updating.

## How to override the default

For a server consuming the shared resource, set `spec.version` in the team server
patch, typically `apps/<team>/<env>/aso/<team>-postgres.yaml`. For example, after
the coordinated API rollout:

```yaml
apiVersion: dbforpostgresql.azure.com/v20250801
kind: FlexibleServer
metadata:
  name: ${NAMESPACE}-${ENVIRONMENT}
  namespace: ${NAMESPACE}
spec:
  version: "16"
```

Keep other settings unchanged and ensure the patch matches the shared resource
and is included in the application's Kustomization.

## Preparing an in-place upgrade

Follow Microsoft upgrade guidance [here](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/postgresql/flexible-server/concepts-major-version-upgrade), including compatibility checks, backups and downtime planning. Confirm the ASO upgrade mechanism before applying; changing spec.version alone has not yet been verified. Afterwards, check the server version, preserved data and application connectivity.

## Example

See [Toffee's upgrade-test PR #9060](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-flux-config/pull/9060/files),
which changes the existing test server's `spec.version` from `"14"` to `"17"`.
The [upgrade-test manifest](https://github.com/hmcts/sds-flux-config/blob/master/apps/toffee/sbox/aso/toffee-postgres-upgrade.yaml)
shows the resulting configuration. This demonstrates the Git change; deployed
version and data-preservation checks are still needed to confirm upgrade success.
